Here's a quick overview of Bogart.

What they are
A French small-cap (Euronext: JBOG) that both makes fragrance and runs beauty stores. It has two divisions:
- Fragrances & Cosmetics (c.18% of revenue): own and licensed brands including Carven, Jacques Bogart, Aholic, Stendhal and Méthode Jeanne Piaubert.
- Beauty Retail (c.82%): c.400 selective perfumeries and drugstores (April, DI, HC Parfümerie, Fann) across Belgium, Germany, France, Slovakia, Luxembourg and the UAE. The network was built by rolling up distressed chains, including Nocibé stores, HC Parfümerie and Planet Parfum.
The company is controlled and run by David Konckier.

Financials
- FY25: revenue €264m (-8.5%), EBITDA €27m including IFRS 16 but only €1.7m pre-IFRS 16, net loss €(22.6)m.
- H1 26: revenue €114m (-7.4%), EBITDA €7.2m, net loss €(14.8)m. Gross margin is holding at 52%, so the problem is volume and rent, not pricing.
- The brands division is the healthier part: H1 EBITDA rose to €3.3m, driven by the niche lines.

Balance sheet is under strain
- Net debt (ex-leases) is €90m and gross cash has fallen from €37m to €13m. A bank debt restructuring is expected around the end of 2026 or early 2027.
- An auditor resigned and the H1 report has been pushed to December. Shares are down c.55% YTD.

The Marionnaud deal
Konckier is building a retail group outside the listed company. Through his personal vehicles he has:
- bought Pieper (110 German stores) in April;
- taken April Israel out of Bogart in October, at an EV of c.€10m (c.0.27x sales), a related-party deal;
- closed Marionnaud (c.€650m revenue, 687 stores, loss-making) last week. The price is undisclosed; there are unconfirmed reports of €1.
That puts c.1,150 doors under family control, with listed Bogart increasingly acting as the brand supplier.

Relevance to our mandate
- These deals set a distressed floor for store-led beauty retail, which helps our argument that value sits with digitally native models.
- I don't see Bogart as a competing bidder; it has no capacity to pay. The family might be one in a distressed or carve-out situation, given their omnichannel ambitions.
- They now control a meaningful share of selective fragrance distribution in France, Benelux and Germany, which is worth covering in commercial diligence.

